

Access Control List:
====================
sometimes you need to give specific permissions to a particular user or group without changing the file's owner or group. That's where ACL is useful.


-rw-r--r--+
          ↑
        ACL exists

Note:
====
Think of the mask as a filter:
1. If the mask allows more than the ACL entry, the ACL entry determines the effective permissions.
2. If the mask allows less than the ACL entry, the mask restricts the effective permissions.

Mathematically: Effective permissions= ACL entry "intersection"  Mask .  Here, intersection means the permissions both entries allow.




# to add permissions 
setfacl -m u:alice:rw report.txt        --> alice acl will be to added to file
setfacl -m g:developers:rw report.txt   --> developers group acl will be to added to file

setfacl -Rm u:alice:rw folder      --> alice acl will be applied to folder and all existing files & subdirectories
setfacl -Rm g:developers:rw folder   --> developers group acl will be applied to folder and all existing files & subdirectories


# View ACL
getfacl report.txt/folder         --> to list acl for file 


# Remove ACL for user
setfacl -x u:alice report.txt       --> alice acl will be to removed to file
setfacl -x g:developers report.txt     --> developers group acl will be to removed to file

setfacl -Rx u:alice folder       --> alice acl will be removed to folder and all existing files & subdirectories
setfacl -Rx g:developers folder    --> developers group acl will be removed to folder and all existing files & subdirectories




# Default ACL for directories
setfacl -m d:u:alice:r /var/www/project    --> d: = default/inheritance ACL → existing objects (files and directories) are not directly modified. New objects (files and directories) created inside those directories can inherit the default ACL.
setfacl -m d:g:testgroup:r /var/www/project

-R → Starting directory + all existing subdirectories recursively; existing regular files inside starting directory and subdirectories are not given the default ACL.
setfacl -Rm d:u:alice:r /var/www/project   --> The default ACL is applied recursively to the existing directories under /var/www/project, not to existing regular files. New objects (files and directories) created inside those directories can inherit the default ACL.
setfacl -Rm d:g:testgroup:r /var/www/project



# to remove default ACL for directories
setfacl -x d:u:alice /var/www/project      --> It removes: alice default acl for directory
setfacl -x d:g:testgroup /var/www/project  --> it removes: testgroup default acl for directory

setfacl -Rx d:u:alice /var/www/project      --> It removes: alice default acl for directory recursively
setfacl -Rx d:g:testgroup /var/www/project  --> It removes: testgroup default acl for directory recursively



getfacl /var/www/project



# remove all extended ACL entries 
setfacl -b file          --> for file
setfacl -b directory     --> for directory, it won't remove for inside folders
setfacl -Rb directory    --> recursive, it will remove for inside folders also


# remove default ACL entries
setfacl -k directory    --> to remove default ACL for directory
setfacl -Rk directory    --> to remove default ACL for directory recursively, it will remove inside folders also


# remove both extended ACL entries & default ACL entries
setfacl -bk directory    --> to remove default ACL & extended ACL for directory
setfacl -Rbk directory    --> to remove default ACL & extended ACL for directory recursively


# to find all files & directories which have acl
find / -exec getfacl -s {} + 2>/dev/null | grep '^# file:'     -->  -s — skips files that have only the base ACL entries (no extended ACL entries).
